    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called over stdio. The pinned `mcp==2.2.0` is SDK v2, where the high-level decorator class is `MCPServer` (from `mcp.server`). I checked the v2 docs before writing the code.\n\n- **Server:** `MCPServer(\"notes\")` is created and returned. The `__main__` block still calls `build_server().run()`, which serves over stdio by default.\n- **`add_note(title, body)`:** stores the note in `NOTES[title]` and returns `\"saved <title>\"`.\n- **`word_count(title)`:** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`. For an unknown title it raises `ToolError(f\"no such note: {title!r}\")`. The v2 docs say to use `ToolError` for failures the model should see; a plain `MCPError` would now come back as a protocol-level error instead.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- Calling the tools from a real stdio client:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\")` returned `saved a`.\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` returned `3`.\n  - `word_count(\"zz\")` came back with `is_error=True` and the message `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: 'zz'`.\n\nNo dependencies were added. I couldn't delete `_check.py`, a temporary script I wrote in the project root for that client check, because the delete command needed approval. Please delete it yourself or approve the removal."
